# The Fire He Lit Is Burning
Tamil title: வேகுதே வைத்த தீ

The Marwari-Gujarati bloc hisses as though stung by a red scorpion. Alagappa, the great benefactor of the South, cries out in anguish. Raja Sir Annamalai carries his burden of supplication to Delhi.

The fire was lit by comrade Liaquat Ali Khan, General Secretary of the Muslim League, which had been called "a league for protecting nawabs, an assembly sustaining imperialism, and a band of office hunters."

The Great War has ended, and years have passed. Yet peace prevails in no part of the world. Repairing the disorder produced in politics and economics remains a complicated undertaking. In every country, those benevolent magnates, those sons of Lakshmi, who for generations plundered the town to feed their furnaces and lived lives of pleasure, are engaged in the noble labour of destroying by every means within their power the workers' new world.

Recognising that capitalism can no longer bring prosperity to people's lives, every European country is moving rapidly along another path. The people of those countries possess the conviction that "life is meant to be lived." With enthusiasm and unity, they are reshaping the pre-war capitalist order in favour of the poor and humble. Facing the opposition that arises, they have taken possession of the seat of government even at the cost of shedding their blood and giving their lives.

To contemplate the state and course of our vast Indian land, caught for so long in the grip of imperialism, its life withered and its people roaming like walking corpses bereft of even the thought of living, is to invite heartburn deliberately.

The imperialist deceiver says grandly, "We are leaving," while its cajoling words and daily manoeuvres only deepen the political tangle here. On one side stands Congress, roaming in pursuit of power and exploiting the people's backwardness and abundant devotion so that, by virtue of its advantages, it may proclaim loudly, "We alone possess the authority to rule the country." On the other stands the Muslim League, saying, "Where we are the great majority, responsibility for government belongs to us." Between the two lie both concealed hostility, like fire covered in ash, and affection.

On another side comes the rustling of the rulers of the princely states, figures one ought to learn about in history books. Elsewhere is a conspiracy that starves the worker, writes false accounts to evade government taxes, and makes repeated, exhausting journeys to America and England in order to join its class counterparts there, multiply its accumulated money many times over, and devise ways to continue starving the workers of our land for profit. On still another side, workers cry in feeble voices and tears that they need a place to live, clothing to wear and food to eat.

In our own province is the pitiable spectacle of Prakasam Pantulu, spindle and mortar in hand, caught in the schemes of his associates, carrying a volcanic heart and the weapons of repression, and conducting a government of lathi blows.

In these circumstances, the budget of comrade Liaquat Ali Khan, Finance Member of the Government of India, brings consolation and inspires confidence in the future. He estimates the Government of India's income for the coming year at 279.5 crore rupees and expenditure at 328 crores.

He has abolished the salt tax outright. The source states that this causes a loss of 8.6 crores in revenue. The income-tax threshold for business profits has been raised from 2,000 to 2,500 rupees, granting a small concession and causing a revenue loss of twenty-five lakhs. The text consequently places the total loss of revenue at fifty-seven crores. Expenditure must somehow be balanced.

What did Liaquat Ali do? Did he set out to squeeze the middle classes and the poor and tax them? No, not at all.

How much false propaganda had been spread about the Muslim League? Was it not reviled as an instrument of imperialism? Were not its leaders abused as representatives of landlords and landed magnates? Was it not said that offices and titles were their sole ambition? Were they not crowned with epithets such as "obstacles to freedom"?

Liaquat Ali has shown the world that none of these accusations applies to them.

He sought out those who possessed money. He resolved to break open the strongboxes of merchant princes who had converted the people's necessities into money on the black market. He appointed a committee to examine the irregular devices adopted by capitalists to escape the excess-profits tax. He imposed a tax on income from capital acquired with wealth accumulated because of the war.

Why say more? He has prepared the ceremonial offering for those "Indians" who transformed into gold, wealth and factories the lives of the many lakhs who died in the Bengal famine, including infants whose breath still carried the fragrance of milk.

The Marwari-Gujarati bloc hisses as though stung by a red scorpion. Alagappa, the great benefactor of the South, cries out in anguish. Raja Sir Annamalai carries his burden of supplication to Delhi.

The Finance Member has also planned to restrain the dishonest currency-exchange racket, something very difficult for us to understand yet fundamental to the capitalist economic order. Naturally, the Birlas and Dalmias are disturbed.

Let us see which new taxes have thrown the capitalists into confusion. Business profits above one lakh rupees are taxed at four annas in the rupee. This is estimated to yield thirty crore rupees. Changes to income tax, taxation of returns on capital and similar categories are estimated to yield another ten crores. Even after all this, it has been decided to cover the remaining expenditure deficit of seventeen crores through borrowing.

He has also made provision for constructive work. Twenty-five crore rupees have been allotted to the Damodar Valley project, which will enrich Bengal and Bihar. More than the amount printed in the damaged source as “1716 crores” has been allotted as subsidy for imported food and for increasing domestic food production. Five crores have been set aside as grants for development work in the provinces, and thirty-two crores as loans. A plan has also been drawn up to build ten thousand homes for coal-mine workers in Bengal and Bihar.

Did the capitalists and merchants who amassed crores during the war welcome the excess-profits tax with praise? What tricks did they not employ to evade it? Did they not agitate to abolish the tax?

Liaquat Ali's answer to those lamenting that these taxes will harm future industrial growth is noteworthy. He explained that the levy would apply only during the coming year and therefore could not be treated as the full design for future industrial policy. It is no surprise that capitalism, founded upon living by exploiting society, cannot understand the Finance Member's social justice. The two are directly opposed philosophies.

Some write and say that the Finance Member produced such a budget only because the League has joined Congress, on the principle that even fibre acquires fragrance when kept with a flower. Yet the Railway Member too, appointed by Congress and formerly part of the Tata machinery of exploitation, sits in the same council.

9 March 1947

---NOTES---
Several figures in the OCR are damaged or internally inconsistent. The translation preserves clearly legible figures, identifies the likely decimal in 279½ and 8.6 crores, and explicitly marks “1716 crores” as a damaged source reading rather than silently guessing a replacement. “ரூபாய்க்குக் கால் ரூபாய்” is rendered as four annas in the rupee, the period-equivalent rate of one quarter. The article's praise concerns Liaquat Ali Khan's 1947 budget and retains its contemporary political terminology.
